<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Imperial Russia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian Empire was the state ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov dynasty</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s proclamation of the empire in 1721 until the revolution of 1917. It expanded across eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to become one of the largest empires in history. Its expansion into Central Asia also made it a strategic factor in the calculations of neighbouring powers, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-empire.jpg" alt="Russian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter the Great · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The foundations of a unified Russian state were laid in the 15th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>. Moscow became the leading Russian principality, and by the early 16th century the remaining Russian states were united with it. As the only independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> power after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1453, its rulers took symbolic steps towards empire: they married into the Byzantine imperial dynasty, adopted the double-headed eagle and took the title of tsar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> was crowned tsar in 1547. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> (r. 1682 to 1725) transformed the tsardom into an empire, fought wars that made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> a major European power, and moved the capital to the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> came to power in a coup against her husband Peter III and presided over further expansion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was partitioned between 1772 and 1815 and most of its land and population came under Russian rule, and by 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was the most populous state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, ahead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Alexander I helped defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-alliance">Holy Alliance</a>. By 1812 Russia had also gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and some territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and it administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> between 1799 and 1867. Most of the empire's growth in the 19th century came from gains in central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><p>In 1853 to 1856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>; the defeat, formalised in the Treaty of Paris of 1856, forced it to demilitarise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. After the 1905 Revolution Nicholas II authorised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a>, though ultimate authority remained with him. Russia entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1914 on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>; high casualties and shortages of food and fuel led to unrest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 brought the abdication of Nicholas II and a Provisional Government, which proclaimed a republic in September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At its height in the late 19th century the empire covered about 22,800,000 square kilometres, roughly one-sixth of the world's landmass and the third-largest empire in history after the British and Mongol empires. By the 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the population was 126 million, with more than 100 ethnic groups, ethnic Russians making up about 45 per cent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> overthrew the Provisional Government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>, and after winning the Civil War against the Whites established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> across most of the former Russian territory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eastern Armenia had been part of the <b>Russian Empire</b> since the treaties of Gulistan in 1813 and Turkmenchay in 1828, and Armenian Marxism remained a minority movement until 1917.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained part of Qajar Iran until the Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, when the treaties of Gulistan and Turkmenchay ceded the khanates north of the Aras River to the <b>Russian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1772 and 1795 Prussia, the Austrian Empire and the <b>Russian Empire</b> divided the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth in three Partitions of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Advised by Grigory Potemkin, Catherine II proclaimed the annexation of Crimea to the <b>Russian Empire</b> on 8 April 1783, and Şahin Giray abdicated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Law was codified in three Statutes of Lithuania (1529, 1566 and 1588), among the most advanced legal codes of their time; the third remained in force in parts of the <b>Russian Empire</b> until 1840.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…the end of the grand duchy as a political form and the beginning of the Tsardom of Russia, which conquered Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan in 1556, became the <b>Russian Empire</b> under Peter the Great in 1721 and passed on Moscow's autocratic traditions to the modern Russian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
